Once it's fully built, call new_for_loop_node->accept(analyzer);

Why Step 3 Works (The Magic):

The ASTAnalyzer::visit(ForLoop&) method is already built to handle this.

It will automatically call symbol_table_.enter_scope().

It will analyze and register the iterator __vec_i in that new, inner scope.

It will then call accept() on the loop's body (BlockStatement).

This will, in turn, call accept() on the AssignmentStatement, which will analyze the ArrayAccess (__vec_result[__vec_i]) and the BinaryOp (vec1[__vec_i] + vec2[__vec_i]).

The analyzer will successfully find all the symbols (__vec_result, __vec_i, vec1, vec2) because they are now correctly registered in their respective scopes.

Finally, visit(ForLoop&) will call symbol_table_.exit_scope().

Analyze the Final Assignment:

Create the LetDeclaration for LET result = __vec_result.

Call let_result_node->accept(analyzer);

By following these steps, all the newly created nodes will be fully analyzed, typed, and registered in the SymbolTable by the time the NewCodeGenerator pass begins. The "Integration Challenge" will be solved, and the NewCodeGenerator will find the symbols (__vec_i, __vec_result, etc.) just like any other variable.

